.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a dynamic window manager for X. This build is a modified dwm in which
windows are managed floating by default: they are moved and resized freely with
the mouse or the keyboard, and windows are never tiled behind your back.
.P
Instead of switchable layouts this build offers a
.B task view
and a set of
.B snap
commands. Task view tiles every window of the current tag at once as an
overview; clicking a window leaves task view and focuses it, restoring the
previous geometry of all windows. The snap commands place a window on the left
or right half of the screen, or place the focused window and the one below it
side by side.
.P
A window can be raised permanently above all others (always on top), or shrunk
into a small tile in the lower right corner of the screen (stay low), which
keeps it visible and clickable while it is out of the way. Successive stay low
windows are stacked with a small offset so they remain distinguishable.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags.
.P
Each screen contains a small status bar which displays, from left to right, the
available tags, the layout symbol, the number of windows on the current tag as
.BR #n ,
the snap indicator
.B [S]
when two windows are snapped side by side, the title of the focused window, and
the text read from the root window name property. The layout symbol is
.B ><>
normally and
.B [T]
while the current tag is in task view. A floating window is indicated with an
empty square before the window title, a fixed size window with a filled square,
and an always on top window with a second square below it. The selected tags
are indicated with a different color. The tags of the focused window are
indicated with a filled square in the top left corner. The tags which are
applied to one or more windows are indicated with an empty square in the top
left corner.
.P
dwm draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.TP
.B X root window name
is read and displayed in the status text area. It can be set with the
.BR xsetroot (1)
command.
.SS Keyboard commands
.TP
.B Mod1\-r
Spawn
.BR dmenu (1)
for launching other programs.
.TP
.B Mod1\-a
Start a terminal with a preset size, font and colour scheme. Each one is placed
slightly below and to the right of the previous one.
.TP
.B Mod1\-c
Start the screenshot tool in region mode.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-Return
Start a terminal.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-p
Lock the screen and turn the display off.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-u
Run the wordlist input helper configured in config.h.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-i
Run the webshell input helper configured in config.h.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-F6
Decrease backlight brightness.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-F7
Increase backlight brightness.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-F8
Reset backlight brightness to its default level.
.TP
.B Mod1\-b
Toggles bar on and off.
.TP
.B Mod1\-w
Focus previous window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-w
Focus next window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Tab
Focus the window that was focused before the current one. Pressing it
repeatedly alternates between the two most recently used windows.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Return
Zooms/cycles focused window to/from master area (task view only).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-c
Close focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-g
Toggle focused window between tiled and floating state.
.TP
.B Mod1\-f
Toggle focused window between its normal geometry and the full screen area
below the bar.
.TP
.B Mod1\-q
Toggle always on top for the focused window. At most one window per tag is
always on top, so this clears the flag on any other window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-v
Toggle stay low for the focused window: shrink it into a small tile in the lower
right corner of the screen and focus the window below it, or restore its
previous geometry.
.TP
.B Mod1\-d
Toggle task view for the current tag. Entering it tiles all windows of the tag,
widening the master area when there are more than three; leaving it restores
every window to its previous geometry.
.SS Snapping
.TP
.B Mod1\-j
Snap focused window to the left half of the screen.
.TP
.B Mod1\-k
Snap focused window to the right half of the screen.
.TP
.B Mod1\-e
Toggle snapping the focused window and the window below it side by side. Both
windows return to their previous geometry when toggled off. This leaves task
view first if it is active.
.TP
.B Mod1\-s
Swap the two side by side snapped windows.
.SS Moving and resizing with the keyboard
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-h
Move focused window left by
.B movestep
pixels (config.h, 30 by default).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-l
Move focused window right by
.B movestep
pixels.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-j
Move focused window up by
.B movestep
pixels. While two windows are snapped side by side this widens the focused
window by
.B snapwidthstep
pixels (config.h, 15 by default) instead.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-k
Move focused window down by
.B movestep
pixels. While two windows are snapped side by side this narrows the focused
window by
.B snapwidthstep
pixels instead.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-d
Increase width of focused window by
.B resizestep
pixels (config.h, 30 by default). The top left corner stays in place.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-e
Decrease width of focused window by
.B resizestep
pixels.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-s
Increase height of focused window by
.B resizestep
pixels.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-q
Decrease height of focused window by
.B resizestep
pixels.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-Shift\-h, \-l, \-j, \-k
Move focused window 100 pixels in that direction. These are examples of move
bindings that carry their own distance instead of using
.BR movestep :
in config.h, writing
.B MOVEBY(DirLeft, 100)
where a plain move binding writes
.B {.ui = DirLeft}
sets the distance for that key. The distance replaces
.B snapwidthstep
too, so while two windows are snapped side by side these keys adjust the split
by 100 pixels rather than by 15. Rebind or delete them to taste.
.P
The keyboard move and resize commands are ignored in task view, for fullscreen
windows, and, except for the snapped case described above, for windows snapped
side by side.
.SS Tags
.TP
.B Mod1\-[1..n]
View all windows with nth tag.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-[1..n]
Add/remove all windows with nth tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-[1..n]
Apply nth tag to focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-Shift\-[1..n]
Add/remove nth tag to/from focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-t
Toggles to the previously selected tags.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-]
Quit dwm.
.SS Mouse commands on the bar
.TP
.B Button1
click on a tag label to display all windows with that tag, click on the layout
symbol toggles task view.
.TP
.B Button2
click on the window title zooms that window to the master area (task view
only), click on the status text starts a terminal.
.TP
.B Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes all windows with that tag to/from the view,
click on the layout symbol toggles task view.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button1
click on a tag label applies that tag to the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes that tag to/from the focused window.
.SS Mouse commands on windows
.TP
.B Button1
click on a window focuses it. Clicking a window in task view additionally
leaves task view and restores every window to its previous geometry. The click
itself is passed on to the window.
.TP
.B Button2
Move focused window while dragging. Tiled windows will be toggled to the
floating state.
.TP
.B Button1 plus Button3
Use both mouse buttons inside a window, in either order, to pick that window
up: hold them down together, or click one and then the other within
.B chordtimeout
milliseconds (config.h, 500 by default). The window is focused, raised, and
from then on follows the pointer with no button held down. Click any button to
put it down where it is; pressing any dwm key binding puts it down as well.
.P
dwm never grabs the pointer for this, so the clicks that pick a window up and
put it down also reach the client underneath as ordinary clicks. Set
.B mousechordmove
to 0 in config.h to disable the gesture. It needs XInput2, see
.B XINPUT2FLAGS
in config.mk; without it dwm prints a warning at startup and the gesture stays
off.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button2
Toggles focused window between floating and tiled state.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button3
Resize focused window while dragging. The corner of the quadrant the pointer is
in is the one that moves, the opposite corner stays anchored. Tiled windows will
be toggled to the floating state.
.P
Dragging a window past the left or right edge of the screen snaps it to that
half, and dragging a snapped window away restores the size it had before it was
snapped.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
dwm is customized by editing config.h and (re)compiling the source code. This
keeps it fast, secure and simple. config.h is generated from config.def.h only
when it does not exist yet, so an existing config.h has to be edited directly.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR slock (1),
.BR xsetroot (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
Send all bug reports with a patch to hackers@suckless.org.
